C = [
("h1", "8. Signs in Acts 9 to 28"),
("p", "Claim: Acts reports signs under the hands of Paul and his companions from Cyprus to Troas, and the text reports them without a program label. The volume reports them as the book reports them. They are recorded in settings shaped by Israel’s Prophecy Program, which remained operative throughout Acts 9 to 28 even as the Mystery Program began. Acts 9 to 28 is the overlap; where Paul or his companions are the ones working signs, the volume reports the text and does not assign the event to a single program."),
("sub", "8.1 Cyprus: the deputy and the sorcerer"),
("p", "At Paphos the deputy of the island wants to hear the word, and Elymas the sorcerer opposes. “Then Saul, (who also is called Paul,) filled with the Holy Ghost, set his eyes on him, And said, O full of all subtilty and all mischief, thou child of the devil, thou enemy of all righteousness, wilt thou not cease to pervert the right ways of the Lord? And now, behold, the hand of the Lord is upon thee, and thou shalt be blind, not seeing the sun for a season” (Acts 13:9–11). The sign is judgment on an opponent. The result follows: “Then the deputy, when he saw what was done, believed, being astonished at the doctrine of the Lord” (Acts 13:12). The text names the effect, belief, and the object of the astonishment, “the doctrine of the Lord.”"),
("sub", "8.2 Iconium and Lystra"),
("p", "At Iconium Paul and Barnabas go into the synagogue, and the account sets the signs beside their speech. “Long time therefore abode they speaking boldly in the Lord, which gave testimony unto the word of his grace, and granted signs and wonders to be done by their hands” (Acts 14:3). The Lord gives testimony “unto the word of his grace” and grants the signs. The signs are described again as an attestation of a word, and the word is called a word of grace."),
("p", "At Lystra the man is a Gentile cripple who has never walked. “The same heard Paul speak: who stedfastly beholding him, and perceiving that he had faith to be healed, Said with a loud voice, Stand upright on thy feet. And he leaped and walked” (Acts 14:9, 10). The account gives the occasion, Paul’s speaking; the perception, “that he had faith to be healed”; and the act. The crowds then take Paul and Barnabas for gods, which the text reports and which the apostles refuse."),
("sub", "8.3 Jerusalem, Philippi, Ephesus and Troas"),
("p", "At the council in Jerusalem the multitude hears the missionaries’ report. “Then all the multitude kept silence, and gave audience to Barnabas and Paul, declaring what miracles and wonders God had wrought among the Gentiles by them” (Acts 15:12). The signs are reported in the presence of the elders and apostles as part of the missionaries’ account, and the account is of what God had wrought “among the Gentiles.”"),
("p", "At Philippi a girl with a spirit of divination follows Paul and his company, and Paul turns on the spirit. “But Paul, being grieved, turned and said to the spirit, I command thee in the name of Jesus Christ to come out of her. And he came out the same hour” (Acts 16:18). At Ephesus the account is the widest in the book: “And God wrought special miracles by the hands of Paul: So that from his body were brought unto the sick handkerchiefs or aprons, and the diseases departed from them, and the evil spirits went out of them” (Acts 19:11, 12). The passage names God as the agent, Paul’s hands and body as the instrument, and the effect as the departure of diseases and evil spirits."),
("p", "At Troas a young man falls from a window and is taken up dead. “And Paul went down, and fell on him, and embracing him said, Trouble not yourselves; for his life is in him. … And they brought the young man alive, and were not a little comforted” (Acts 20:10, 12). The account gives Paul’s act and the result. It does not say that Paul prayed or that anyone expected the result."),
("sub", "8.4 How the signs were received"),
("p", "The crowd at Lystra reads the healing as a visit of gods. “And when the people saw what Paul had done, they lifted up their voices, saying in the speech of Lycaonia, The gods are come down to us in the likeness of men” (Acts 14:11). The priest of Jupiter brings oxen and garlands. The apostles run in among the people: “Sirs, why do ye these things? We also are men of like passions with you, and preach unto you that ye should turn from these vanities unto the living God” (Acts 14:14, 15). Paul and Barnabas turn the sign from themselves to the God who made heaven and earth. The crowd wants to worship the workers, and the workers refuse."),
("p", "At Ephesus the name of the Lord Jesus is used by men who have no commission. “Then certain of the vagabond Jews, exorcists, took upon them to call over them which had evil spirits the name of the Lord Jesus, saying, We adjure you by Jesus whom Paul preacheth.” The spirit answers, “Jesus I know, and Paul I know; but who are ye?” and the man overcomes them, “so that they fled out of that house naked and wounded” (Acts 19:13, 15, 16). The result is public: “And this was known to all the Jews and Greeks also dwelling at Ephesus; and fear fell on them all, and the name of the Lord Jesus was magnified” (Acts 19:17). The account closes with many who confess and burn their books: “So mightily grew the word of God and prevailed” (Acts 19:20). The passage places the working of signs with those whom God sends, and it ties the effect to the growth of the word."),
("sub", "8.5 What the text reports and what it leaves unsaid"),
("p", "The passages are consistent in their form. The signs follow speech: at Paphos, at Iconium, at Lystra. They are done in the name of Jesus Christ (Acts 16:18), by the hands of Paul (Acts 19:11), and are said to be granted by the Lord (Acts 14:3). The people who see them are Jews, Gentiles and a deputy of Rome; some believe, and some refuse."),
("p", "The passages leave several things unsaid. They do not say that the signs are the content of Paul’s message. They do not say that the signs are promised to everyone who believes Paul’s message. They do not say that the signs will continue after the book closes. And they do not label the signs as belonging to the Prophecy Program or the Mystery Program. The volume does not supply the label. The book of Acts is the record of the overlap, and Paul’s signs are recorded in it."),
("ctbl", "Signs in Acts 9 to 28, as reported.", [
    ("Passage", "Where and by whom", "What the text reports"),
    ("Acts 13:9–12", "Paphos; Paul", "Elymas blinded; the deputy believed"),
    ("Acts 14:3", "Iconium; Paul and Barnabas", "The Lord testified to the word of His grace and granted signs and wonders"),
    ("Acts 14:8–10", "Lystra; Paul", "A man who never walked stood and walked"),
    ("Acts 15:12", "Jerusalem; Barnabas and Paul", "Miracles and wonders reported among the Gentiles"),
    ("Acts 16:16–18", "Philippi; Paul", "A spirit commanded in the name of Jesus Christ to come out"),
    ("Acts 14:11–15", "Lystra; the crowd and the apostles", "The crowd takes them for gods; the apostles refuse and turn them to the living God"),
    ("Acts 19:11, 12", "Ephesus; Paul", "Special miracles; diseases and evil spirits departed"),
    ("Acts 19:13–20", "Ephesus; the sons of Sceva", "The name used without commission; the name magnified; the word grew"),
    ("Acts 20:9–12", "Troas; Paul", "A young man taken up dead was brought alive"),
  ], [0.20, 0.30, 0.50]),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Acts reports signs under Paul’s hands from Cyprus to Troas, with the Lord named as the agent.",
    "The signs follow speech and are described as testimony to the word.",
    "At Lystra the apostles refuse worship, and at Ephesus the name is magnified and the word grows.",
    "The volume reports these signs as the book reports them and assigns them to no single program.",
    "The text does not say the signs belong to the Mystery or that they are promised to those who believe Paul’s message.",
    "The text does not say they continue after the book closes.",
  ]),
]
